{"parentUuid":"d915a665-e9cd-43a7-a030-fd12088de42c","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"eb903157-6eb9-4dd8-8710-2e428622c046","agentId":"ac5c8f6946e43cacc","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LRLT8TtsYPPPcGQWu2MhJ9","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"CODD solver decision diagram ECAI 2024 combinatorial optimization van Hoeve\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"CODD: A Decision Diagram-based Solver for Combinatorial Optimization\",\"url\":\"https://www.andrew.cmu.edu/user/vanhoeve/papers/CODD-ECAI2024.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"IOS Press Ebooks - CODD: A Decision Diagram-Based Solver for Combinatorial Optimization\",\"url\":\"https://ebooks.iospress.nl/doi/10.3233/FAIA240997\"},{\"title\":\"dblp: CODD: A Decision Diagram-Based Solver for Combinatorial Optimization.\",\"url\":\"https://dblp.org/rec/conf/ecai/MichelH24.html\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) CODD: A Decision Diagram-Based Solver for Combinatorial Optimization\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/385033522_CODD_A_Decision_Diagram-Based_Solver_for_Combinatorial_Optimization\"},{\"title\":\"dblp: Willem Jan van Hoeve\",\"url\":\"https://dblp.uni-trier.de/pid/61/5378.html\"},{\"title\":\"An Introduction to Decision Diagrams for Optimization\",\"url\":\"https://www.andrew.cmu.edu/user/vanhoeve/papers/DD_TutORial.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"An Introduction to Decision Diagrams for Optimization | Tutorials in Operations Research: Smarter Decisions for a Better World | Tutorials in OR\",\"url\":\"https://pubsonline.informs.org/doi/10.1287/educ.2024.0276\"},{\"title\":\"Parallel Combinatorial Optimization with Decision Diagrams\",\"url\":\"http://www.cs.toronto.edu/~horst/cogrobo/papers/ParallelDD.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"INFORMS 2024 © 2024 INFORMS|isbn 978-0-xxxxxxx-x-x\",\"url\":\"https://optimization-online.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/DD_TutORial_2024.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Decision Diagrams for Optimization - CMU\",\"url\":\"https://www.andrew.cmu.edu/user/vanhoeve/mdd/\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found comprehensive information about CODD and its presentation at ECAI 2024:\n\n## CODD: A Decision Diagram-Based Solver for Combinatorial Optimization\n\nCODD is a system for solving combinatorial optimization problems using decision diagram technology, authored by Laurent Michel and Willem-Jan van Hoeve, and was presented at ECAI 2024.\n\n### Key Features\n\nProblems are represented as state-based dynamic programming models using the CODD language specification, and the model specification is used to automatically compile relaxed and restricted decision diagrams that are embedded inside a branch-and-bound search process.\n\nThe system introduces abstractions that allow generic implementation of the solver components while maintaining overall execution efficiency.\n\n### Design Innovations\n\nOne notable design aspect of CODD is its layer-free approach: Decision diagrams are usually presented as stratified graphs where nodes are organized in layers, but CODD creates diagrams as directed graphs where layers are implicit and resemble 'stages' as in dynamic programming.\n\n### Performance\n\nCODD demonstrates functionality on a variety of combinatorial optimization problems and provides competitive results that can outperform other state-based solvers as well as integer programming and constraint programming solvers, sometimes by orders of magnitude.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"015872c8-9f57-43ae-842c-1a5c9b0bcf7d","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:06:28.383Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"d915a665-e9cd-43a7-a030-fd12088de42c","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"61a2fb0a-54bd-4d04-bab7-c81b7b1d1364","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"eb903157-6eb9-4dd8-8710-2e428622c046","agentId":"ac5c8f6946e43cacc","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LL9mvrUgaxBCLbECezzMxe","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"decision diagram lazy clause generation LCG comparison constraint programming\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"[2307.08242] Lifted Sequential Planning with Lazy Constraint Generation Solvers\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.08242\"},{\"title\":\"Lazy Clause Generation! A powerful hybrid solving approach\",\"url\":\"https://school.a4cp.org/summer2011/slides/Gent/Peter%20Stuckey%20-%20Lazy%20Clause%20Generation.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Symmetries and Lazy Clause Generation ∗ Geoffrey Chu and Peter J. Stuckey\",\"url\":\"https://www.ijcai.org/Proceedings/11/Papers/094.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Lazy Clause Generation: Combining the Power of SAT and CP (and MIP?) Solving\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/221353593_Lazy_Clause_Generation_Combining_the_Power_of_SAT_and_CP_and_MIP_Solving\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Symmetries and Lazy Clause Generation.\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/220814076_Symmetries_and_Lazy_Clause_Generation\"},{\"title\":\"Conflict Directed Lazy Decomposition | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-642-33558-7_8\"},{\"title\":\"Lazy Clause Generation Reengineered | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/221633085_Lazy_Clause_Generation_Reengineered\"},{\"title\":\"Implicit Conflict-Based Search Using Lazy Clause Generation\",\"url\":\"https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/ICAPS/article/download/3471/3339/6520\"},{\"title\":\"Lazy Clause Generation Reengineered | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-642-04244-7_29\"},{\"title\":\"Parallel Planning using a Lazy Clause Generation Solver | IEEE Conference Publication | IEEE Xplore\",\"url\":\"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/9288164/\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found relevant information about decision diagrams, lazy clause generation (LCG), and their relationship in constraint programming. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Lazy Clause Generation (LCG) Overview\n\nLazy Clause Generation (LCG) is an approach to Constraint Programming used for tackling sequential classical planning. More broadly, lazy clause generation is a powerful hybrid approach to combinatorial optimization that combines features from SAT solving and finite domain propagation.\n\nLCG combines a finite domain CP solver with a SAT solver by mapping finite domain propagators to clauses in a SAT solver.\n\n## Decision Diagrams and LCG\n\nThe research literature reveals a connection between decision diagrams and LCG. A generic method can learn valid clauses from exact or approximate binary decision diagrams (BDDs), with any clause learned from SAT conflict analysis also being generatable using this method, while also generating stronger clauses that cannot be derived from one application of conflict analysis.\n\nSince SAT instances are often too large for an exact BDD representation, the focus is on BDD relaxations of polynomial size that can still be used to generate useful clauses.\n\n## Performance Comparison\n\nWhen comparing approaches, constraint programming, particularly with lazy clause generation (LCG), has been found to be faster than Mixed Integer Programming (MIP) for some scheduling problems with time-varying action costs.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"7762b23a-171d-4cff-bcd9-cbba25eb0533","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:06:29.183Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"61a2fb0a-54bd-4d04-bab7-c81b7b1d1364","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"6f09f352-e009-4848-b396-b3f30493f3a8","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"eb903157-6eb9-4dd8-8710-2e428622c046","agentId":"ac5c8f6946e43cacc","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YaRPYTU61ziz5MFEpZ5uer","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"ZDD zero suppressed decision diagram packing placement combinatorial\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Zero-suppressed decision diagram - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zero-suppressed_decision_diagram\"},{\"title\":\"Zero-Suppressed Decision Diagrams\",\"url\":\"https://www.emergentmind.com/topics/zero-suppressed-decision-diagram-zdd\"},{\"title\":\"Applications of Zero-Suppressed Decision Diagrams | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-3-031-79870-2\"},{\"title\":\"Introduction to Zero-Suppressed Decision Diagrams | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-79870-2_1\"},{\"title\":\"BDDs - Binary Decision Diagrams\",\"url\":\"https://crypto.stanford.edu/pbc/notes/zdd/\"},{\"title\":\"An Introduction to Zero-Suppressed Binary Decision Diagrams Alan Mishchenko\",\"url\":\"https://people.eecs.berkeley.edu/~alanmi/publications/2001/tech01_zdd_.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) A Zero-Suppressed Binary Decision Diagram Approach for Constrained Path Enumeration\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/334285757_A_Zero-Suppressed_Binary_Decision_Diagram_Approach_for_Constrained_Path_Enumeration\"},{\"title\":\"Zero-Suppressed Sentential Decision Diagrams\",\"url\":\"https://cdn.aaai.org/ojs/10114/10114-13-13642-1-2-20201228.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Zero-Suppressed Decision Diagrams and Independent Sets | Rahul Nair\",\"url\":\"https://rahulnair23.github.io/post/decision-diagrams/\"},{\"title\":\"Applications of Zero-Suppressed Decision Diagrams | Springer eBooks | IEEE Xplore\",\"url\":\"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/book/6977799\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, here's what I found about ZDDs (zero-suppressed decision diagrams) and their applications to combinatorial problems like packing and placement:\n\n## Overview of ZDDs\n\nA zero-suppressed decision diagram (ZDD) is a particular kind of binary decision diagram (BDD) with fixed variable ordering that provides a canonically compact representation of sets, particularly suitable for certain combinatorial problems.\n\n## Applications to Combinatorial Problems\n\nZDDs are especially suitable for combinatorial problems, such as the classical problems in two-level logic minimization, knight's tour problem, fault simulation, timing analysis, the N-queens problem, as well as weak division.\n\nMore broadly, a zero-suppressed decision diagram (ZDD) is a data structure to represent objects that typically contain many zeros, with applications including combinatorial problems, such as graphs, circuits, faults, and data mining.\n\n## Relevance to Packing and Placement\n\nZDDs, often hybridized with ternary decision diagrams (TDDs), represent graph partitions subject to complex constraints (e.g., minimum component weights), enabling enumeration orders-of-magnitude faster than previous approaches. Additionally, ZDDs efficiently solve sparse combinatorial problems, such as minimal vertex covers and subgraph enumeration, using memoized recursion and compression techniques.\n\nThese capabilities suggest ZDDs could be applicable to packing and placement problems that involve constraint satisfaction and combinatorial enumeration of feasible configurations.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"6935bcfe-cec9-4855-b462-cdc951b54b22","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:06:29.466Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"6f09f352-e009-4848-b396-b3f30493f3a8","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":